# API Standards

## API Response Format

All API routes must return the `ApiResponse<T>` format:

```typescript
interface ApiResponse<T> {
  success: boolean;
  data?: T;
  error?: string;
  timestamp: string;
}
```

## Authentication

- All protected routes MUST use `requireAuth()` middleware
- Place auth check at the top of the handler
- Return early if authentication fails

## Error Handling

- Always use try-catch blocks
- Return appropriate HTTP status codes:
  - 400: Bad Request (validation errors)
  - 401: Unauthorized (auth failures)
  - 403: Forbidden (permission denied)
  - 404: Not Found
  - 500: Internal Server Error
- Use logger for errors, not console.error

## Request Validation

- Validate all inputs
- Return clear error messages
- Use Zod schemas for complex validation (when implemented)

## Example API Route

```typescript
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from "next/server";

import { requireAuth } from "@/lib/auth";
import { logger } from "@/lib/logger";

import { ApiResponse } from "@/types";

export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  // 1. Authentication
  const authError = requireAuth(request);
  if (authError) return authError;

  try {
    // 2. Parse and validate input
    const body = await request.json();
    // ... validation ...

    // 3. Business logic
    const result = await performOperation(body);

    // 4. Success response
    return NextResponse.json({
      success: true,
      data: result,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    } as ApiResponse<ResultType>);
  } catch (error) {
    // 5. Error handling
    logger.error("POST /api/endpoint failed", { error });
    return NextResponse.json(
      {
        success: false,
        error: "Operation failed",
        timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      } as ApiResponse<null>,
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}
```
